It's like in a dynamic
equilibrium when that happens.

00:14:34.979 --> 00:14:38.939
So when we say that water is free, it's
because it can move where it wants.

00:14:39.625 --> 00:14:41.824
However, we're talking about
colligative properties.

00:14:42.165 --> 00:14:44.594
So what happens if I add a solute?

00:14:44.685 --> 00:14:49.925
When we add a solute, water favors
dissolving it, meaning that if I have

00:14:50.004 --> 00:14:52.915
NACL, it will want to dissolve it.

00:14:52.945 --> 00:14:54.505
It will rush to it.

00:14:54.745 --> 00:14:56.765
It will clump around it.

00:14:57.104 --> 00:15:01.815
So what will happen is if I
have, let's say an area of high

00:15:01.875 --> 00:15:03.485
salt concentration on the left.

00:15:03.495 --> 00:15:04.555
So you see the purple.

00:15:04.919 --> 00:15:06.310
And the green dots here.

00:15:06.540 --> 00:15:07.510
So there's more salt.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:11.020
So what's going to happen
here as a result, the water

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.140
is going to rush through it.

00:15:12.449 --> 00:15:17.010
And if you remember this semi permeable
membrane, it allows water to pass.

00:15:17.369 --> 00:15:21.809
So all of the water is going
to go from an area of low salt

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:24.469
to high salt concentration.

00:15:24.469 --> 00:15:25.550
So it's all going to go.

00:15:26.135 --> 00:15:30.105
in towards towards the
left hand side as a result.

00:15:30.445 --> 00:15:32.395
So let's see what this
is going to look like.

00:15:32.445 --> 00:15:35.895
So what you're going to see is
after that raises all the water

00:15:35.895 --> 00:15:37.815
all of a sudden is all on the left.

00:15:37.855 --> 00:15:39.635
Notice how much higher it is.

00:15:39.945 --> 00:15:42.724
So if you can imagine this as
a cell, it's going to be a lot

00:15:42.834 --> 00:15:44.675
plumper of a cell as a result.

00:15:45.025 --> 00:15:46.715
So it's going to all build up.

00:15:46.785 --> 00:15:49.275
This is known as osmotic pressure.

00:15:49.485 --> 00:15:52.065
When it all goes on one entire side.

00:15:52.365 --> 00:15:56.125
So this is why you need to make sure
you regulate your salt and sugar

00:15:56.125 --> 00:16:00.815
levels in your body because water
can rush from one area to another.

00:16:01.105 --> 00:16:04.514
So if you're diabetic or if maybe
you have a very high salt levels in

00:16:04.514 --> 00:16:07.745
your body and you need to watch your
food, this is one of the, this is the

00:16:07.745 --> 00:16:11.475
reason why, because you can imagine
how this might be applied to cells.

00:16:11.935 --> 00:16:13.974
So let's take a look at
applying this to biology then.

00:16:14.014 --> 00:16:17.045
When you eat or drink, one thing
that's going to happen is that

00:16:17.045 --> 00:16:20.734
your intestines are going to act
like a semi permeable membrane.

00:16:21.194 --> 00:16:25.615
So generally what they'll do is, is
they will take out all the water,

00:16:25.685 --> 00:16:29.935
all the water that you drink in
will get brought into the body.

00:16:30.204 --> 00:16:34.295
So here I'm kind of showing how
water is flowing into the body.

00:16:34.295 --> 00:16:38.005
So usually whenever you drink
a lot of water, what's going to

00:16:38.005 --> 00:16:41.555
happen is your body tends to have
a higher concentration of solutes.

00:16:41.844 --> 00:16:44.724
So it's going to flow out, it's
going to hydrate your cells.

00:16:45.474 --> 00:16:48.175
But, what about, why do you
always drink fresh water?

00:16:48.194 --> 00:16:52.724
Why can't we drink salt water when there's
so much of it on our planet, right?

00:16:52.754 --> 00:16:56.264
97 percent of the water is salt
water, wouldn't it be great if you

00:16:56.264 --> 00:16:57.354
could just drink it the way it is?

00:16:57.755 --> 00:17:02.125
Well, the reason is why you can't
do it is because of osmosis.

00:17:02.565 --> 00:17:06.915
So if you were drinking a very
salty liquid, so think of if your

00:17:06.925 --> 00:17:10.095
intestines were full of Salty water.

00:17:10.505 --> 00:17:14.695
What's going to happen is when,
because of osmosis, it's going

00:17:14.695 --> 00:17:18.225
to flow from an area of low
concentration to high concentration.

00:17:18.685 --> 00:17:24.115
So if your body, for example, is
considered to be low concentration,

00:17:24.555 --> 00:17:27.794
so let's just say low and what
we have on the inside is high.

00:17:28.645 --> 00:17:29.995
So we'll write high here.

00:17:31.035 --> 00:17:35.925
It's going to all blow out of
your body and dehydrate you.

00:17:36.245 --> 00:17:39.315
So if you ever drink salt water, not
only does it taste gross, but you're

00:17:39.315 --> 00:17:42.315
going to start feeling extremely
sick because all of your cells

00:17:42.384 --> 00:17:45.075
are now do not have enough water.

00:17:45.315 --> 00:17:47.865
And this can be a really big
problem with red blood cells.

00:17:47.895 --> 00:17:48.945
Let's take a look at that.

00:17:48.985 --> 00:17:52.435
And when it comes to your blood, your
blood cells are something that can

00:17:52.435 --> 00:17:56.645
be extremely affected by the amount
of salt and sugar in your body.

00:17:57.024 --> 00:18:00.504
So when you're giving IVs to
maybe a patient in the hospital.

00:18:00.814 --> 00:18:03.764
They need to make sure that the
levels are appropriate for your

00:18:03.764 --> 00:18:07.694
patient so that way their cells
are healthy and hydrated and happy.

00:18:08.205 --> 00:18:10.334
So one thing that you're going
to see here is there's three

00:18:10.334 --> 00:18:11.534
blood cells in front of you.

00:18:11.975 --> 00:18:13.974
We have an isotonic cell.

00:18:14.384 --> 00:18:19.665
Now what that refers to is that the level
of essentially the concentration of salt

00:18:19.764 --> 00:18:22.224
and sugar are normal inside the cells.

00:18:22.745 --> 00:18:24.274
and outside the cell.

00:18:24.655 --> 00:18:28.025
So what's going to happen is
pretty much the water is going to

00:18:28.025 --> 00:18:31.435
flow in and out nice and happily.

00:18:31.435 --> 00:18:33.405
There's going to be no issues at all.

00:18:34.214 --> 00:18:36.924
The one on the top in the
middle here is known as a

00:18:36.924 --> 00:18:40.405
hypotonic, a hypotonic situation.

00:18:40.894 --> 00:18:47.195
So this is when you have essentially a
very, very low sugar or salt in the body.

00:18:47.685 --> 00:18:52.605
So let's say if I kind of write,
uh, next to it is if I have low

00:18:53.444 --> 00:18:56.760
sugar, in the body, so around it.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.260
So it's in a hypotonic solution.

00:18:59.630 --> 00:19:04.479
Well, what's going to happen is inside
of your cell is typically always 0.

00:19:04.540 --> 00:19:06.879
9 and 5%.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:13.109
So let's say if outside
the cell, it was like 0.

00:19:13.110 --> 00:19:14.200
4%, and 4.

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:18.060
5 percent sugar, salt
and sugar respectively.

00:19:18.629 --> 00:19:23.759
What's going to happen is all of
the water that is now outside is

00:19:23.780 --> 00:19:26.500
going to rush inside of the cell.

00:19:26.500 --> 00:19:27.510
It's all going to come in.

00:19:28.050 --> 00:19:32.170
Now, when that happens, your cell is
going to blow up like a big balloon

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:36.580
and as a result it could pop and end up
actually killing your cell essentially

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.280
if you have a too big of an imbalance.

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:43.549
So for people who might be diabetic
and have really low blood sugar, this

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:46.199
is one of the reasons why they're
feeling very lethargic is because their

00:19:46.199 --> 00:19:48.089
cells are not functioning properly.

00:19:48.089 --> 00:19:49.890
It's not at the right concentration.

00:19:50.250 --> 00:19:52.450
So it's very important to regulate levels.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.200
Now the other option is,
let's say if I have my cell.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:20:02.820
And let's say on the, uh, inside of the
cell is normal and everything's happy.

00:20:02.820 --> 00:20:03.310
So we have 0.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:06.660
9 percent and 5 percent
for salt and sugar.

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:13.399
Now if the outside is, let's say you
drink a lot of two of really salty foods

00:20:13.770 --> 00:20:18.390
and you have a lot of different sugar and
cake and all kinds of really good stuff.

00:20:18.855 --> 00:20:21.525
Well, what's going to happen
here is the opposite effect.

00:20:21.705 --> 00:20:24.805
Remember from high, or
sorry, from low to high.

00:20:25.145 --> 00:20:29.085
So when that happens, we have
really low concentration inside

00:20:29.455 --> 00:20:31.425
and everything's going to come out.

00:20:31.854 --> 00:20:37.965
Therefore we have a hypertonic cell, which
is when it's going to dehydrate your cell

00:20:38.064 --> 00:20:42.805
and cause it to collapse in on itself,
losing its function and its purpose.

00:20:43.294 --> 00:20:46.874
So this is some of the factors that can
happen because of colligative properties.

00:20:47.445 --> 00:20:48.515
So let's sum it up.

00:20:48.605 --> 00:20:51.935
So to sum up everything, the most
important concepts that you need to

00:20:51.935 --> 00:20:56.444
know is first is counting how many
particles that you have when you

00:20:56.495 --> 00:20:58.415
write your solubility equations.

00:20:58.425 --> 00:21:03.025
So you have to write how they dissociate
and fall apart when they're dissolved.

00:21:03.685 --> 00:21:08.414
Depending on the number of particles,
the more of them the more of an impact

00:21:08.485 --> 00:21:12.664
that your, that your solute will
have on the property of the solution.

00:21:13.414 --> 00:21:15.364
Now, next would be molality.

00:21:15.384 --> 00:21:16.485
So that'd be the second thing.

00:21:16.965 --> 00:21:20.865
So if the particles are the same,
check on the concentration next.

00:21:21.374 --> 00:21:26.475
Um, and if they're both the same, then you
look at if it's an ionic molecule versus

00:21:26.504 --> 00:21:29.544
covalent, ionic has the stronger impact.

00:21:30.435 --> 00:21:34.555
Now, when we're looking at what
properties it has, The solute will

00:21:34.555 --> 00:21:38.755
lower the solution's freezing point, so
we can have freezing point depression.

00:21:39.315 --> 00:21:41.375
Now, like I said, there's
proportional molality.

00:21:42.175 --> 00:21:45.894
Your solute can also raise the solution's
boiling point, so that's why we add

00:21:45.914 --> 00:21:50.054
antifreeze, that way it helps prevent
your car from overheating and also can

00:21:50.054 --> 00:21:51.775
also prevent it from freezing up as well.

00:21:52.644 --> 00:21:55.794
And then the last one is that
solutes can draw water through

00:21:55.794 --> 00:21:57.645
membranes, meaning pickling, right?

00:21:57.645 --> 00:21:59.544
So pickling or antifreeze or any of these.

00:22:00.004 --> 00:22:03.784
So you have freezing point depression,
boiling point elevation, and

00:22:03.784 --> 00:22:06.905
osmosis, and that's what you need
to know for colligative properties.

